course: dictionary
lesson: 4472
title: Harmony: notes and people fitting together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Harmony means things fitting together well. In music, it is when different notes are played or sung at the same time and sound pleasant. «Harmony means singing different notes at the same time that sound good together.» The clue is in the words "sound good together".
explain: Harmony also means people getting along peacefully. «Living in harmony» means living together without fighting, even when people are different. To check, swap in your meaning: "living together with no fighting" makes sense here, so you have the right meaning.
explain: A harmony, with an a and an s, is a noun you can count. It is a second tune sung with the main one to make it sound richer. «A choir often splits into parts singing different notes at once, called harmony, so a different note is not always a mistake.»
fact: Harmony means things fitting together peacefully or pleasantly.
fact: In music, harmony is different notes played or sung together that sound pleasant.
fact: A harmony is a second tune sung with the main one to make it sound richer.
fact: «Multitrack recording puts each part on its own track, so one singer can record many layers of harmony.»
fact: In nature, an ecosystem is in harmony when plants and animals are in balance.
exercise: «Harmony means singing different notes at the same time that sound good together.» What does harmony mean here? | Singing with no music at all | Singing the same note over and over | Singing one note very loudly | *Different notes sung together that sound pleasant
exercise: «The two neighbours live in harmony, even though they speak different languages.» What does living in harmony mean? | Arguing about everything every day | Living in a very big house | *Getting along peacefully despite differences | Moving far away from each other
exercise: A singer sings the main tune, and her friend sings a second tune on top. What is the friend's second tune called? | *A harmony | A rhythm | A key | A chorus
test: «Multitrack recording puts each part on its own track.» In this sentence, what is each part put on? | A piece of paper | The floor | *Its own track | One big track for everyone
test: Which use of harmony means people getting along? | *Living in harmony with the neighbours | A harmony sung above the main tune | Singing in harmony in the choir | Two notes played together on the piano
exam: Ella says harmony is a second tune, and her teacher says it means peace. Who is right about the meaning? | Neither is right, because it is a type of food | Only Ella is right, because it is only about music | *Both are right, because harmony has more than one meaning | Only the teacher is right, because it is only about people
